WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.082
Windows forensics is the preservation and analysis of evidence from Windows systems.

00:00:06.082 --> 00:00:15.769
Those systems may be employee laptops, desktops, servers, virtual machines, cloud-hosted Windows instances, or specialized systems that support business operations.

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:26.357
The purpose is to understand what happened, when it happened, what accounts or systems were involved, and what evidence supports those conclusions.

00:00:26.357 --> 00:00:30.638
Windows forensics is a core part of DFIR.

00:00:30.638 --> 00:00:38.522
It supports incident response, insider investigations, malware triage, policy investigations, user activity reconstruction, and breach analysis.

00:00:38.522 --> 00:00:50.462
A Windows system can contain evidence in event logs, user profiles, registry hives, file system metadata, execution traces, security tool telemetry, application data, and memory.

00:00:50.462 --> 00:00:58.797
The analyst's job is to preserve and correlate those sources, not to rely on a single artifact.

00:00:58.797 --> 00:01:11.188
Authorization and careful handling matter because Windows endpoints often contain sensitive business data, personal information, credentials, browser activity, chat history, source code, documents, and regulated records.

00:01:11.188 --> 00:01:17.270
Forensic collection should follow approved authority, case scope, and data handling rules.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:26.056
The analyst should know what is being collected, why it is needed, and how it will be protected.

00:01:26.056 --> 00:01:28.984
Evidence integrity is the foundation.

00:01:28.984 --> 00:01:35.067
Investigator actions can alter timestamps, create logs, change memory, or overwrite artifacts.

00:01:35.067 --> 00:01:41.149
Sometimes live response is necessary, but it should be deliberate and documented.

00:01:41.149 --> 00:01:45.430
Strong Windows forensics is not about memorizing tools.

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:53.314
It is about preserving context, interpreting artifacts carefully, building timelines, and communicating confidence and limitations clearly.
